</code></pre></div><p>Well, in fact, this isn&rsquo;t an ideal approximation, it&rsquo;s almost exactly <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stylewarning/lisp-random/refs/heads/master/nbody/impl.lisp">how it turned out</a>. Given this is a proof of concept, we sometimes have to write some Lisp things a little funny. For example, you&rsquo;ll notice we write:</p>
<pre tabindex="0"><code>(+ (+ (* dx dx) (* dy dy)) (* dz dz))
</code></pre><p>instead of the far more readable</p>
<pre tabindex="0"><code>(+ (* dx dx) (* dy dy) (* dz dz))
</code></pre><p>Both are completely valid and both can be used. So why the former? It is a result of a limitation of a little feature I built in: auto-vectorization. The vectorizer walks the mathematical expressions and replaces them with fast SIMD variants instead. Here&rsquo;s a little fragment showing this rewrite rule:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0"><code class="language-lisp"><span style="color: #f92672;">...</span>
